findings.body:
`act()` always sets `primary_state_change_event_id`. A firing always has at least one trigger state, so the ID is never None. It passes that ID to every `TriggeredAction`, including the ones for `automation.actions`, which use `firing.triggering_event`. For a proactive trigger with no matching events, `firing.triggering_event` is `None`.

The new validator rejects exactly that combination:

::diff{file="src/prefect/server/events/schemas/automations.py" lines="772-780"}

So `TriggeredAction(...)` raises a `ValidationError` inside the list comprehension in `act()`:

::code{file="src/prefect/server/events/triggers.py" lines="473-489"}

::callout{type="warning"}
I reproduced this on the head commit: `TriggeredAction(triggering_event=None, automation_triggered_event_id=uuid4(), ...)` fails with "automation_triggered_event_id can only be set when triggering_event is provided". In production, a proactive automation like "alert me if no heartbeat event arrives in 10 minutes" would emit `automation.triggered` and then crash before publishing any actions. Those are the cases where the automation matters most.

The existing `test_acting_publishes_an_action_message_from_a_proactive_trigger` builds a firing with no triggering event, so it should hit this error. Nothing explains the constraint either: the `automation.triggered` event exists whether or not there was a triggering event. Remove the validator.

::suggestion{file="src/prefect/server/events/schemas/automations.py" lines="772-780"}

In `fail()`, the `related=` arguments were swapped the wrong way. `action.failed` now gets `related_resources`, which is correct. But `action.triggered` was switched from `related_resources` to `self._resulting_related_resources`:

::diff{file="src/prefect/server/events/actions.py" lines="197-206"}

This is a regression. Before this PR, `action.triggered` on the failure path already carried the `triggering-event` link. Now it carries neither that link nor the new `automation-triggered-event` link. It is also inconsistent with `succeed()`, where both events use `related_resources`.

The PR's own `test_failure_events_include_automation_triggered_event_link` asserts that `triggered_event.related` contains `automation-triggered-event`, so that test should fail against this code.

::suggestion{file="src/prefect/server/events/actions.py" lines="202-202"}
                    related=related_resources,

Every action gets `primary_state_change_event_id`, which prefers Triggered. If a firing has both states, an `actions_on_resolve` action gets `triggering_event = resolved_event`, but `automation-triggered-event` points to the *triggered* event:

::diff{file="src/prefect/server/events/triggers.py" lines="466-471"}

That contradicts the docs and the field description, which say the link is "the `automation.triggered` or `automation.resolved` event that prompted the action". OSS code doesn't currently create Resolved firings, so the impact today is small. Still, using the state-specific event's ID costs nothing and matches the docs:

::suggestion{file="src/prefect/server/events/triggers.py" lines="468-471"}
        source_actions += [
            (resolved_event, action, resolved_event.id)
            for action in automation.actions_on_resolve
        ]

Apply the same change to `actions_on_trigger`, using `triggered_event.id`.

Both `act()` tests copy the parsed value back into the expected object, so the comparison always passes:

::diff{file="tests/events/server/triggers/test_service.py" lines="57-61"}

If `act()` left out the ID or set the wrong one, these tests would still pass. That's how the proactive-trigger crash above could slip through without a dedicated assertion. Capture the published `automation.triggered` event (via the `messaging.publish` mock) and assert `parsed.automation_triggered_event_id == that_event.id`.
